<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="book_route.py" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
<metadata><name>Timberline Lodge to White River</name><desc>or-vielbig-2e:130-2: route read from the book's text and sketch map. One trkseg per waypoint pair, in order; segment sources are in the trk desc. Geometry from OSM (ODbL, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors) and USFS EDW (public domain) where marked; 'straight' segments are off-trail connectors, not traced.</desc></metadata>
<wpt lat="45.331129" lon="-121.711243"><ele>1813.1</ele><name>Timberline Lodge</name><desc>source: DB trailhead pin th:timberline-lodge (OSM way 149173768) (copied from or-vielbig-2e_130-1); confidence: high; pdf_page: 132; book_elev_ft: 5950; quote: Climb behind the lodge</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.333800" lon="-121.710000"><ele>1847.8</ele><name>North of the lodge, west lip of the first gully</name><desc>source: about 300 yards north of the lodge (DEM 1,850 m), approach as in the companion tour 'Timberline Lodge to White River' (copied from or-vielbig-2e_130-1); confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: From the northeast corner of the upper parking lot at Timberline Lodge, cross the road and ski northward climbing gently for about 300 yards to a very steep gully. Cross this potentially dangerous gully and head southeast, along contours to another similar gully that also must be crossed. Continue to the near edge of White River Canyon, which is perhaps 700 yards from your starting point.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.334000" lon="-121.707300"><ele>1819.1</ele><name>First gully (Salmon River headwaters) crossing</name><desc>source: DEM low 1,819 m on the ravine at 45.334 (copied from or-vielbig-2e_130-1); confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: From the northeast corner of the upper parking lot at Timberline Lodge, cross the road and ski northward climbing gently for about 300 yards to a very steep gully. Cross this potentially dangerous gully and head southeast, along contours to another similar gully that also must be crossed. Continue to the near edge of White River Canyon, which is perhaps 700 yards from your starting point. / 'cross a very steep ravine to the east. This is the headwaters of the Salmon River'</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.333600" lon="-121.704500"><ele>1818.1</ele><name>Second gully crossing</name><desc>source: DEM low 1,821 m on the second gully at 45.334; reached southeast along the contours (copied from or-vielbig-2e_130-1); confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: From the northeast corner of the upper parking lot at Timberline Lodge, cross the road and ski northward climbing gently for about 300 yards to a very steep gully. Cross this potentially dangerous gully and head southeast, along contours to another similar gully that also must be crossed. Continue to the near edge of White River Canyon, which is perhaps 700 yards from your starting point.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.333000" lon="-121.703000"><ele>1806.1</ele><name>Near edge of White River Canyon (top of the ridge)</name><desc>source: DEM 1,806 m, about 150 m west of where the canyon wall drops (1,723 m at 45.332, -121.701); 0.65 km (about 700 yards) from the lodge (copied from or-vielbig-2e_130-1); confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: From the northeast corner of the upper parking lot at Timberline Lodge, cross the road and ski northward climbing gently for about 300 yards to a very steep gully. Cross this potentially dangerous gully and head southeast, along contours to another similar gully that also must be crossed. Continue to the near edge of White River Canyon, which is perhaps 700 yards from your starting point.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.331000" lon="-121.703000"><ele>1770.1</ele><name>Ridge crest 1</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.329000" lon="-121.702500"><ele>1767.9</ele><name>Ridge crest 2</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.327000" lon="-121.701000"><ele>1732.4</ele><name>Ridge crest 3</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.325000" lon="-121.699500"><ele>1689.9</ele><name>Ridge crest 4</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.323000" lon="-121.698500"><ele>1651.9</ele><name>Ridge crest 5</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.321000" lon="-121.697000"><ele>1613.7</ele><name>Ridge crest 6</name><desc>source: DEM crest (local high across the slope) of the ridge west of the White River floor, 100 m cross-section scan; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Follow the crest of a ridge downhill, keeping White River Canyon on your left. ... The ridge soon broadens as it continues its gradual descent, offering wide, shallow bowls for long traverses.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.320000" lon="-121.696000"><ele>1594.6</ele><name>Ridge at about 5,200 ft: drop into the canyon</name><desc>source: DEM crest at about 5,240 ft (the book's 5,200); confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; book_elev_ft: 5200; quote: Continue to about 5,200 feet (0.7 mile), where a descent may be made on steep, open slopes into the canyon. If snow appears unstable, continue down into the trees where the descent will be easier and safer.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.319000" lon="-121.684200"><ele>1454.6</ele><name>White River Canyon floor</name><desc>source: vertex of the OSM White River line (or-vielbig-2e_183-1_river.geojson) below the drop; confidence: medium; pdf_page: 132; quote: Once on the flat floor of White River Canyon, follow it to the White River bridge by way of either the left or right side, where a bench with scattered trees descends to the Gravel Pit, then to the bridge.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.303657" lon="-121.674434"><ele>1305.0</ele><name>White River West Sno-Park, at the White River bridge</name><desc>source: DB trailhead pin th:white-river-west-sno-park (OSM way 40773253); the Hwy 35 bridge over the White River is 250 m east; confidence: high; pdf_page: 132; book_elev_ft: 4223; quote: Once on the flat floor of White River Canyon, follow it to the White River bridge by way of either the left or right side, where a bench with scattered trees descends to the Gravel Pit, then to the bridge.</desc></wpt>
<trk><name>Timberline Lodge to White River</name><desc>1. Timberline Lodge -&gt; North of the lodge, west lip of the first gully: straight (medium), 0.19 mi | 2. North of the lodge, west lip of the first gully -&gt; First gully (Salmon River headwaters) crossing: straight (medium), 0.13 mi | 3. First gully (Salmon River headwaters) crossing -&gt; Second gully crossing: straight (medium), 0.14 mi | 4. Second gully crossing -&gt; Near edge of White River Canyon (top of the ridge): straight (medium), 0.08 mi | 5. Near edge of White River Canyon (top of the ridge) -&gt; Ridge crest 1: straight (medium), 0.14 mi | 6. Ridge crest 1 -&gt; Ridge crest 2: straight (medium), 0.14 mi | 7. Ridge crest 2 -&gt; Ridge crest 3: straight (medium), 0.16 mi | 8. Ridge crest 3 -&gt; Ridge crest 4: straight (medium), 0.16 mi | 9. Ridge crest 4 -&gt; Ridge crest 5: straight (medium), 0.15 mi | 10. Ridge crest 5 -&gt; Ridge crest 6: straight (medium), 0.16 mi | 11. Ridge crest 6 -&gt; Ridge at about 5,200 ft: drop into the canyon: straight (medium), 0.08 mi | 12. Ridge at about 5,200 ft: drop into the canyon -&gt; White River Canyon floor: straight (low), 0.58 mi | 13. White River Canyon floor -&gt; White River West Sno-Park, at the White River bridge: line (medium), 1.30 mi</desc>
